---
/**
 * /mission — what this project is, where it's going, and how to help.
 *
 * This is the "about" page with teeth. Not a corporate mission statement —
 * a genuine explanation of what we're trying to build and an invitation
 * to anyone who cares about the same things.
 */

import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { stats } from "../lib/concepts.ts";
import { allTopics } from "../lib/topics.ts";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Mission — NIAC Atlas"
  description="What we're building, why it matters, and how to help."
  nav="mission"
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl">
    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    {/* Header                                                      */}
    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    <header class="mb-16 hero-glow">
      <div class="eyebrow text-accent mb-3">The mission</div>
      <h1
        class="font-display text-5xl md:text-6xl font-semibold tracking-tight leading-[1.05]"
      >
        Make all of human scientific knowledge browsable, connected, and alive.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-8 text-lg text-dim leading-relaxed">
        NASA's website is old and clunky. arXiv is a firehose. Wikipedia is
        flat. Google Scholar is just search. Nowhere on the internet can you
        start with a black hole, follow a thread through 300 years of physics,
        watch the best explanation ever filmed, read the original paper, and
        discover that a NASA-funded team is working on something related
        right now — all in one place.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-dim leading-relaxed">
        We're building that place.
      </p>
    </header>

    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    {/* Where we are                                                */}
    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    <section class="mb-20">
      <div class="eyebrow text-accent mb-4 pb-2 border-b hairline">
        Where we are now
      </div>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Right now, NIAC Atlas indexes <strong class="text-accent">{stats.totalConcepts} speculative aerospace concepts</strong> from
        NASA's Innovative Advanced Concepts program — fusion engines, solar
        sails, gravitational lensing telescopes, submarine explorers for
        Titan's methane seas. Each one is a real idea that someone got
        funded to seriously study.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
        We have <a href="/explore" class="text-accent underline hover:text-white">{allTopics.length} curated deep dives</a> into
        the big scientific questions these concepts touch: black holes,
        gravity, quantum physics, propulsion, telescopes. Each one pulls
        together history, open questions, the best explainer videos and books
        we've found, and the NIAC concepts pushing the field forward.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed">
        It's a start. There's a long way to go.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    {/* Where we're going                                           */}
    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    <section class="mb-20">
      <div class="eyebrow text-accent mb-4 pb-2 border-b hairline">
        Where we're going
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-8">
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold mb-2">
            A universal index of scientific knowledge
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base text-dim leading-relaxed">
            Every paper, every book, every dataset that matters — modern and
            historical. We want a search for "rotating tether" to return
            NIAC concepts, arXiv papers, historical texts, and the best
            explainer videos on the topic. We're starting with NASA. Next:
            arXiv, OpenAlex, PubMed. Eventually: the deep history —
            Galileo's notebooks alongside JWST observations.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold mb-2">
            Connections that help people discover
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base text-dim leading-relaxed">
            AI-assisted semantic search and automatic cross-referencing so the
            platform actively surfaces relationships nobody has drawn before.
            A 2024 JWST observation connects to a 1905 Einstein paper connects
            to a 1687 Newton manuscript. The system should help you think, not
            just search.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold mb-2">
            A community that builds knowledge together
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base text-dim leading-relaxed">
            The most interesting discourse on the internet happens when
            curious, intelligent people gather around things they genuinely
            care about. We want that here — not engagement-optimized noise,
            but the kind of conversation that makes you smarter for having
            read it. Discussion, debate, shared research, and the possibility
            that two people find each other here and go on to work on
            something that matters.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold mb-2">
            The best explanations, linked to primary sources
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base text-dim leading-relaxed">
            Science communicators like Veritasium, 3Blue1Brown, PBS Space
            Time, the Dwarkesh Podcast, Quanta Magazine, and many others do
            extraordinary work making hard ideas accessible. Their
            explanations should live alongside the papers and data they draw
            from — so you can go from "that was a great video" to "here's
            the actual paper" in one click.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl font-semibold mb-2">
            History as a first-class citizen
          </h3>
          <p class="text-base text-dim leading-relaxed">
            You cannot understand the present without understanding the past.
            Every modern discovery stands on centuries of prior work. We want
            to make those lineages visible — so you can see that the concept
            of a "dark star" predates Einstein by 130 years, that Newton's
            reflecting telescope begat Hubble begat JWST, that the rocket
            equation Tsiolkovsky wrote in 1903 is still the constraint every
            mission designer fights today.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    {/* How to help                                                 */}
    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    <section class="mb-20">
      <div class="eyebrow text-accent mb-4 pb-2 border-b hairline">
        How to contribute
      </div>

      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed mb-8">
        This is an open-source project and we need help. Here are real things
        you can do right now, no matter your background.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        <div
          class="p-6 rounded-lg border hairline"
          style="background: var(--color-paper-raised);"
        >
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="text-accent font-mono text-lg shrink-0">01</span>
            <div>
              <h4 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold mb-1">
                Curate a topic deep dive
              </h4>
              <p class="text-sm text-dim leading-relaxed">
                Pick a scientific topic you're passionate about. Write a short
                overview, list the open questions, find the best videos and
                books, and trace the historical timeline. We'll help you get
                it into the site. You don't need to be an expert — you need
                to be curious and thorough.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div
          class="p-6 rounded-lg border hairline"
          style="background: var(--color-paper-raised);"
        >
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="text-accent font-mono text-lg shrink-0">02</span>
            <div>
              <h4 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold mb-1">
                Improve the data
              </h4>
              <p class="text-sm text-dim leading-relaxed">
                Our NIAC concept data is good but not perfect. Phase metadata
                is ~70% detected via keyword sniffing — the rest needs
                human eyes. Some concepts appear as duplicates across Phase I
                and Phase II reports. If you have domain knowledge, help us
                clean and enrich the dataset.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div
          class="p-6 rounded-lg border hairline"
          style="background: var(--color-paper-raised);"
        >
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="text-accent font-mono text-lg shrink-0">03</span>
            <div>
              <h4 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold mb-1">
                Digitize historical sources
              </h4>
              <p class="text-sm text-dim leading-relaxed">
                The hardest and most important part of the mission. Pre-1900
                scientific texts — manuscripts, early journal articles,
                engineering drawings — need to be scanned, OCR'd, transcribed,
                and linked to modern work. If you read Latin, German, French,
                Arabic, or Chinese, your skills are especially valuable. Even
                English-language historical texts need careful transcription.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div
          class="p-6 rounded-lg border hairline"
          style="background: var(--color-paper-raised);"
        >
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="text-accent font-mono text-lg shrink-0">04</span>
            <div>
              <h4 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold mb-1">
                Write code
              </h4>
              <p class="text-sm text-dim leading-relaxed">
                The project is TypeScript (Astro + React), open source, and
                there's no shortage of engineering work: ingestion pipelines
                for new data sources (arXiv, OpenAlex, PubMed), semantic
                search infrastructure, better visualizations, accessibility
                improvements, and eventually community features. Check the
                repo and pick up an issue.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div
          class="p-6 rounded-lg border hairline"
          style="background: var(--color-paper-raised);"
        >
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
            <span class="text-accent font-mono text-lg shrink-0">05</span>
            <div>
              <h4 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold mb-1">
                Recommend great science communication
              </h4>
              <p class="text-sm text-dim leading-relaxed">
                Know a video, podcast, book, or article that changed how you
                think about a scientific topic? We want to know about it.
                The curated media layer is only as good as the community's
                collective taste. Send us your favorites.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    {/* The belief                                                  */}
    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    <section class="mb-12 pt-12 border-t hairline">
      <blockquote class="pull-quote max-w-2xl">
        "The most exciting phrase to hear in science, the one that heralds
        new discoveries, is not 'Eureka!' but 'That's funny...'"
      </blockquote>
      <p class="mt-4 eyebrow">— Isaac Asimov (attributed)</p>
      <p class="mt-8 text-base text-dim leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
        We believe the next great scientific insight is more likely to happen
        when a curious person can wander freely through all of human
        knowledge — from a 1783 thought experiment about dark stars to a
        2025 NIAC study on gravitational lensing — and notice a connection
        that nobody else has seen. That's what we're building toward.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    {/* CTA                                                         */}
    {/* ----------------------------------------------------------- */}
    <section class="mb-8">
      <a
        href="https://github.com/anthonyguzzardo/niac-atlas"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener"
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-accent eyebrow hover:text-white transition"
      >
        <span>View the project on GitHub</span>
        <span>→</span>
      </a>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
